              {
                "seq": 1,
                "kind": "assistant_message",
                "text": "A **mutex** (mutual exclusion) and a **semaphore** are both synchronization primitives, but they differ in purpose and flexibility:\n\n**Mutex**\n- Binary state: locked or unlocked\n- Only the thread that acquired the lock can release it\n- Prevents race conditions by ensuring only one thread accesses a resource at a time\n- Simpler, more restrictive use case\n\n**Semaphore**\n- Counter-based: holds a numeric value (not just 0/1)\n- Any thread can increment or decrement the counter\n- Can allow multiple threads to access a resource simultaneously (counting semaphore)\n- More flexible — can solve various synchronization problems beyond mutual exclusion\n\n**Key differences:**\n\n| | Mutex | Semaphore |\n|---|---|---|\n| **State** | Binary (locked/unlocked) | Integer counter |\n| **Ownership** | Owned by acquiring thread | No ownership |\n| **Use case** | Protect critical sections | Control access to limited resources |\n| **Count** | Always 1 | Can be any value |\n| **Release** | Must be released by acquirer | Can be released by any thread |\n\n**Example:**\n- **Mutex**: Ensuring only one thread writes to a file at a time\n- **Semaphore**: Limiting a thread pool to 5 concurrent database connections (semaphore initialized to 5)\n\nA **binary semaphore** (counter = 1) is conceptually similar to a mutex, but lacks the ownership guarantee, which can lead to bugs if misused.\n\nIn practice, mutexes are preferred for protecting shared data, while semaphores are better for signaling and resource counting scenarios."
              },
              { "seq": 2, "kind": "session_end", "outcome": "completed" }
            ],
            "env": { "writes": [], "deletes": [], "network": [], "unobserved": [] }
