              {
                "seq": 1,
                "kind": "assistant_message",
                "text": "A **semaphore** and **mutex** are both synchronization primitives, but they have key differences:\n\n## Mutex (Mutual Exclusion)\n- **Binary lock** — can only be locked (1) or unlocked (0)\n- **Ownership** — only the thread that locked it can unlock it\n- **Purpose** — protects shared resources by ensuring only one thread accesses a critical section at a time\n- **Simpler** — designed for straightforward mutual exclusion\n\n## Semaphore\n- **Counter-based** — maintains a count (0, 1, 2, ...)\n- **No ownership** — any thread can increment (signal) or decrement (wait) it\n- **Flexible** — can limit access to multiple resources or coordinate multiple threads\n- **More general** — a mutex is essentially a binary semaphore with ownership semantics\n\n## Practical Differences\n\n| Aspect | Mutex | Semaphore |\n|--------|-------|-----------|\n| **Lock holder** | Must be released by the same thread | Can be released by any thread |\n| **Count** | 0 or 1 only | Any non-negative integer |\n| **Use case** | Protecting critical sections | Resource pools, signaling, coordination |\n| **Example** | Protecting a shared variable | Limiting 3 threads to access 5 resources |\n\n## Example Scenario\n- **Mutex**: A bank account that only one thread can modify at a time\n- **Semaphore**: A parking lot with 10 spaces—any thread can leave (increment) and any waiting thread can enter (decrement)\n\nIn most cases, use a **mutex** for simple resource protection. Use a **semaphore** when you need more complex coordination or multiple resources."
              },
              { "seq": 2, "kind": "session_end", "outcome": "completed" }
